{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787222699,"nonce":"wEkWJtOif3T+D9YL8ZG5D1wezR8=","message":"Stages 6 and 7 done; stages 1–5 landed earlier on this branch. Closing.\n\n**The false clause, recorded so it is not re-derived.** The body says today's generation is smuggled in\nthrough `alert_groups.generation` inside `subject_id`. That is **false** — `subject_id` is plain row\nidentity (`00008`, `00011`), nothing unpacks it, and `generation` was deleted outright rather than\nrelocated. No `channel_threads.generation` was added. The correction is recorded in SPEC §C.4.\n\n**Stage 6 — both axes, built as further axes on the existing gates rather than a parallel mechanism.**\nMigration `00072_a_policy_binds_a_subject_and_counts_it.sql`:\n\n- **`subject_kinds TEXT[]`** (empty = every kind), `reasons`-shaped storage that decides through the\n  *existing* `Policy.Handles` gate — so `PolicyService.Evaluate`, `Digests()` and the preview all honour\n  it with no change to the notification path. Its overlap with `reasons` is **conceded, not hidden**:\n  `Reason.Subject()` is total, so as a pure filter it is derivable from a hand-narrowed reason list. It\n  earns its place as the count's UNIT, and as a declaration an operator can read without knowing the\n  fifteen-entry Reason-to-subject map by heart.\n- **`count_min` / `count_window_s`** — `Throttle`'s dual: a floor where throttle is a ceiling, on a\n  sliding window. Evaluated in `NotificationService.suppressors` beside the throttle, recording the new\n  `below_threshold` reason (migration `00073`, `notifications_suppmap_ck` six → seven).\n\n**It shipped inert twice and both were caught before closing.** First, the evaluator was missing\nentirely — `count_min` was stored, validated and published while suppressing nothing. Then, once wired,\nreview found `subject_kinds=['alert']` + `count_min` was a **permanent mute**: an alert's `subject_id`\nis the alert identity, unchanged across every firing, so `count(DISTINCT subject_id)` never rises and\nevery notification is suppressed forever — and that was precisely the configuration this ticket\nadvertises as the replacement for hardcoded flap detection. Resolved by **refusing** rather than\nreinterpreting: `policies_count_case_ck` plus `validateCount` require `subject_kinds == [\"case\"]`\n(error `subject_kinds/unsupported`), because `fired` is a case-subject Reason and `{case}` already\nexpresses \"five restarts this hour\" honestly, while occurrence-counting for `{alert}` would have\nchanged what an operator observes into something no column states. A `{digest}` binding is refused by\nthe same rule — the digest tick bypasses the suppressor chain, so a count there is read by nothing.\n`policies_digest_subject_ck` likewise refuses a digest policy whose binding omits `digest`, which\notherwise made the tick warn every tick forever *and* the reconciler skip the policy.\n\n**ADR 0044** records the axis; **§5's precedence rank is now owner-ratified (2026-08-20)**. It shipped\nas that change's own proposal — \"ceiling before floor\" appeared in no SPEC clause and no earlier ADR —\nand was labelled as a proposal in four places rather than laundered into spec authority. It is now a\nruling, and ADR 0044 §5 is where its authority lives; SPEC §B.8.2 records it. Overturning it stays\ncheap by construction: one slice literal, one SPEC line, one contract description and that section.\nNothing stored depends on the order.\n\n**Stage 7 — documentation.** `CONTEXT.md`: **AlertGroup** removed from FR-1's signal list and the\nglossary, a new **Conversation** term, ChannelThread re-pointed, the stale `grouping` module swept from\nthe table, import DAG, ports, job table, §2c and §7, and a ⛔ retired-noun block so it cannot come back.\n`SPEC.md`: §C.4 rewritten without reopening §C.3's cut (no ADR supersedes 0038 — stage 2's collapse key\nwas reverted); §D.5's `CREATE TABLE alert_groups` and nine `/api/v1/alert-groups*` routes deleted from\nthe live catalogue, with tombstones where a *ban* rather than an artifact was being recorded; §D given\nthe new tables and columns; §G.3 given the reconciler row.\n\n⚠️ **Left undecided deliberately: SPEC §H.6 acceptance criterion 14** (\"300 alerts → one card\") is now\ninverted — 300 alerts produce 300 conversations. It is annotated, not rewritten, because §C.4 says that\nproduct ruling has not been made. A SPEC that records its own open question beats an agent inventing\nthe answer. **That is the one thing a reader of this ticket should still act on.**","files":null}]}